      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "## Water Ionization Constant (pKw)\n\nThe water ionization constant, pKw, represents the autoprotolysis of water:\n\n**2 H₂O ↔ H₃O⁺ + OH⁻**\n\nThe equilibrium constant is defined as:\n\n**Kw = (H₃O⁺)(OH⁻)**\n\nWhere (H₃O⁺) and (OH⁻) represent the concentrations of hydronium and hydroxide ions respectively.\n\n### Key Points:\n\n- **pKw varies with temperature**: The standard value of 1.00×10⁻¹⁴ (pKw = 14) applies at 25°C\n- **Heavy Water (D₂O)**: Self-ionizes less than normal water, resulting in larger pKw values\n- **Ion Concentrations**: Since H₃O⁺ and OH⁻ are produced in a 1:1 molar ratio: (H₃O⁺) = (OH⁻) = √Kw\n- **Temperature Dependence**: pKw decreases as temperature increases for both H₂O and D₂O"
